<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Roman Numidia</h1><p class="st-meta">198 CE – 439 CE (approx.) · capital Cirta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rome's frontier province in eastern Algeria, home of the Third Augustan Legion, the city of Timgad and the Donatist church.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a> came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a> in 46 BCE and was long administered as part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis</a>, although the commander of the Third Augustan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> effectively governed it. Around 198 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> made it a separate province, governed by the legate from the fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambaesis">Lambaesis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The legion guarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès</a> mountains and the desert frontier, and veterans settled in new towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timgad">Thamugadi</a> (Timgad), founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> in 100. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> was the leading city, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-algeria">Constantina</a> by Constantine. The high plains produced grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a> was the stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatist</a> church and the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> briefly split it into Numidia Cirtensis and Numidia Militiana.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 435 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> took control of parts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a>, and their capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 439 ended Roman rule. Byzantine forces later reoccupied much of it, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kingdoms such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-aures">Kingdom of the Aures</a> held the mountains.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/roman-numidia.jpg" alt="Roman Numidia" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Tomisti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Numidia_(AD_210).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>198 CE – 439 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cirta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Berber, Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Numidia (Roman province), Numidia Cirtensis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=318&amp;focus=roman-numidia" aria-label="Roman Numidia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4846 866 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Roman Numidia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5166 1122L5328 1276M5166 1122L5304 942M5166 1122L5259 1106"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5328 1276h0M5304 942h0M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 1122h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 1122h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cirta. <a href="/atlas?year=318&amp;focus=roman-numidia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
